{"path":"/testbed/migration-recon/reports/reconciliation.json","content":"{
  "source_db": "databases/source.db",
  "target_db": "databases/target.db",
  "scope": ["customers", "orders", "order_items", "payments", "addresses"],
  "tables": {
    "customers": {"source_rows": 8000, "target_rows": 8000, "equal": true},
    "orders": {"source_rows": 50200, "target_rows": 50000, "equal": false},
    "order_items": {"source_rows": 40000, "target_rows": 40000, "equal": true},
    "payments": {"source_rows": 12000, "target_rows": 12000, "equal": true},
    "addresses": {"source_rows": 9000, "target_rows": 9000, "equal": true}
  },
  "matched": false,
  "notes": "Counts from `python scripts/reconcile.py` on the fixed snapshots, with orders evaluated per the contract's rows-on-record definition. Per RECONCILIATION_CONTRACT.md, orders on record = active orders (orders) + archived orders (orders_archive). source.db: orders=50000, orders_archive=200 -> 50200 rows on record; target.db: orders=50000, orders_archive=0 -> 50000 rows on record. The 200 archived orders are missing from target.db entirely (none of their ids appear in target.orders; target.orders_archive is empty), consistent with migrations/0002_orders_backfill.sql skipping legacy rows whose settlement id failed to parse. All other in-scope tables match exactly. Because one in-scope table (orders) differs, the migration is NOT matched."
}
"}
